00:00:33.841 --> 00:00:34.340
Core.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:41.542
Put simply, IoT
is taking devices

00:00:41.542 --> 00:00:43.750
that were not traditionally
connected to the internet

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:46.060
and then adding internet
connectivity to them.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:46.720
Why?

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.030
Because data is being created
all the time from devices,

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:51.850
applications, and their users.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:55.240
By adding connectivity, a wealth
of data that was traditionally

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.553
offline becomes actionable.

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:04.180
HEATHER CROSS: Cloud IoT Core
is a fully managed service

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:07.060
that allows you to easily
and securely connect, manage,

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:09.940
and ingest data from up to
millions of globally dispersed

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:11.314
IoT devices.

00:01:11.314 --> 00:01:12.730
The service is
server-less, and it

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:15.700
doesn't require any upfront
software installation.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.370
It also scales instantly
with the horizontal scaling

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:19.840
of Google Cloud Platform.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.340
JENNY BROWN: Cloud
IoT Core provides

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:22.780
a way of creating
a registry where

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.570
you can define where devices
are and then connect them.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.150
Device telemetry data is
forwarded to a Cloud Pub/Sub

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:29.200
topic.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.230
You can then use Cloud
Functions to trigger API calls,

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:35.260
or you can use DataFlow to
extract that data from Pub/Sub

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.810
and put it into a data
warehouse, such as Bigtable

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:38.874
or BigQuery.

00:01:38.874 --> 00:01:40.540
This will allow you
to analyze and react

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:41.740
that data in real time.

